{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/605393-hierarchy-of-story","id":605393,"title":"Hierarchy of Story","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Additional Discussion","Off-Topic"],"posts":[{"date":"2015-07-28T01:14:25-07:00","id":605393,"title":"Hierarchy of Story","content":"So, I'm tutoring an introductory psych course this semester, and I went along to the first lecture so I can get a feel for the students. During which, the lecturer went briefly over Maslow's Hierarchy of Needs, which got me thinking about the importance of various aspects of story.<br /> <br /> Putting aside how flawed the idea is for a moment, the first level is basic survival stuff. I suppose this'd be analogous to the players being reasonably certain that you won't just kill the characters apropos of nothing. I find it extraordinarily difficult to get involved if the ST just drops hyper-powerful vampires in front of us that we can't escape from, or has a sniper blow our heads off. So, I guess this can be considered a basic requirement. (Granted, this more has to do with trusting the ST, but I'm trying to see if I characterise it differently any insights will result).<br /> <br /> Safety is the next level. I don't know if I'd say this is necessary? A degree, sure, but if you're safe then that makes games difficult?<br /> <br /> It gets harder to relate the higher you go, so let's stop there. The way I'd characterise games Needs are (in ascending order):<br /> <br /> 1. Survival. The players need to know that, barring doing something stupid or an unlucky dice roll, they're unlikely to die in any given session.<br /> 2. Conflict. The game needs something to drive it, something for the characters to oppose.<br /> 3. Growth. The characters need to be able to grow and develop over time. This might mean XP, it might mean emotional/RP development, but if the characters are stationary then it gets stale quick.<br /> 4. Feelings of achievement. The players need to feel like what they do matters, that they are affecting the game world.<br /> 5. A satisfactory conclusion.<br /> <br /> What do you think? Agree/disagree with my ordering/choice? How would you characterise the important elements of a good game/story if we were to use this model?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1mSW2-p-SwmYw5gaqnVMQ7HcRNhVedC-aA4vB6sBclGk/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">My Commandments for GMs</a> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1hslzrmui6ML-v7LniQUXLNyy8o52YF9g2u6usdu-Drc/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">My Commandments for Players</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/207-allan53","name":"Allan53","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"966"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-07-28T05:47:04-07:00","id":605513,"title":"","content":"Sounds like a solid progression of needs, yes. Some would even discount the &quot;unlucky roll&quot; from the list of posible deaths and maybe add that if the the death feels apropiate for a character arc it is fair game. Some consider the final death of a character as a reasonable and expected end that works as an Satisfactory Conclusion emanating from the Growth and Sense of Achievement of the story that led there.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i><b>I</b>'m <b>S</b>o <b>M</b>eta <b>E</b>ven <b>T</b>his <b>A</b>cronym</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/195-thorbes","name":"Thorbes","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar195_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"}